<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thihathu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1325">1325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thihathu (died 1325) was the youngest of three Shan-Burmese brothers who repelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and took power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> after the fall of Pagan. In 1313 he founded a new capital at Pinya and proclaimed himself heir to the Pagan kings. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-yun">Saw Yun</a> broke away to set up a rival court at Sagaing in 1315.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thihathu was born in 1265 into a prominent family of Myinsaing, the third of four children of Theinkha Bo, a younger brother of the sawbwa of Binnaka, and the daughter of a wealthy banker of the town; the chronicles disagree on his birth and death dates. Like his elder brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athinkhaya">Athinkhaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazathingyan">Yazathingyan</a> he entered the service of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a>. From 1285 the three led the defence of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, and in 1287, after the king's assassination, they stopped a Mongol invasion, which withdrew to Tagaung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the anarchy that followed, the brothers held the army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> granary. King Kyawswa appointed them viceroys on 19 February 1293, Thihathu at Pinle, but their own inscription declared them the king's equals. Thihathu, the most ambitious, styled himself Lord of the White Elephant in 1295 and Great Lord in 1296. When Kyawswa sought Mongol protection, the brothers deposed him on 17 December 1297, with the help of the dowager queen Pwa Saw, and installed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-hnit">Saw Hnit</a>; they executed Kyawswa in May 1299.</p><p>A Mongol army of 12,000 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> besieged Myinsaing from 25 January 1301. Its main attack failed, and on 6 April it withdrew after a bribe of 800 taels of gold and 2,200 taels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Thihathu proclaimed himself king on 20 October 1309, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athinkhaya">Athinkhaya</a>'s death on 13 April 1310 he pushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazathingyan">Yazathingyan</a> aside.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Thihathu first chose the site of the later Ava, at the meeting of the Irrawaddy and Myitnge rivers, but rejected it for bad omens and built Pinya a few miles east, opened as Wizayapura on 7 February 1313. At his coronation that day Pwa Saw gave him the golden belt and tray handed down since King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a>. His chief queen was Mi Saw U, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a>, and he named her son by Kyawswa, his adopted son Uzana, heir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-yun">Saw Yun</a>, his son by a commoner queen, left for Sagaing on 16 May 1315 and walled it; two expeditions failed to retake it, and Thihathu let him be.</p><p>The chronicles say he gave his last years to religion and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a>'s heirlooms to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwezigon-pagoda">Shwezigon Pagoda</a>. He died about February 1325, aged 59, and Uzana succeeded him. He is remembered as Tazishin, Lord of One White Elephant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thihathu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His rejection of Yuan demands for tribute brought defeat at Ngasaunggyan in 1277 and the loss of the north in the 1280s; he fled to Lower Burma, earning the name ‘the king who fled from the Chinese’, and was murdered by his son <b>Thihathu</b> at Prome in 1287.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thihathu</b>, who with his brothers had defended central Burma from the Mongols, proclaimed himself successor of the Pagan dynasty and founded the Pinya Kingdom, moving his capital from Pinle to Pinya.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-7">7 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Athinkhaya, Yazathingyan and <b>Thihathu</b>, sons of a Shan chief and commanders under the last Pagan kings, built up power in the Kyaukse district from bases at Myinsaing, Mekkaya and Pinle.</blockquote><a href="/state/myinsaing-kingdom">Myinsaing Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thihathu</b>, the last surviving of the three Myinsaing brothers, founded a new capital at Pinya near the Kyaukse rice lands in 1313 and proclaimed himself heir to the Pagan kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/pinya-kingdom">Pinya Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1315 Saw Yun, a son of <b>Thihathu</b> of the Pinya Kingdom, quarrelled with his father and set up his own court at Sagaing, across the Irrawaddy from present-day Mandalay.</blockquote><a href="/state/sagaing-kingdom">Sagaing Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kyaukse District, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Pinya, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.75,96.25&amp;year=1315" aria-label="Kyaukse District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Thihathu"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5495 5690h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5495 5690h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.75,96.25&amp;year=1315">What was here in 1315? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
